##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## SpyGlass Lint Waiver File
##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver files suppress specific SpyGlass messages that have been reviewed
## and determined to be acceptable. Each waiver MUST include a comment
## explaining why the violation is safe to ignore.
##
## Syntax:
##   waive -rule {RULE_ID}
##     [-instance <hierarchical_path>]
##     [-module <module_name>]
##     [-signal <signal_name>]
##     -comment "Justification"
##
## Best practices:
##   1. Be as specific as possible (instance > module > global)
##   2. Always provide a clear justification in -comment
##   3. Review waivers whenever the RTL changes
##   4. Get senior engineer sign-off for critical waivers
##   5. Version control waiver files alongside RTL
##-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

##=============================================================================
## Example Waivers for Tutorial Designs
##=============================================================================

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 1: Unused debug signal in counter_with_issues
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## The debug_sig signal is intentionally left undriven in the tutorial
## example to demonstrate the W_UNDRIVEN violation. In a real design,
## you might waive debug signals that are only connected during
## FPGA prototyping.
##
## waive -rule {W_UNDRIVEN}
##   -module {counter_with_issues}
##   -signal {debug_sig}
##   -comment "Tutorial example: intentionally demonstrates W_UNDRIVEN"

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 2: Unused internal flag
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Similarly, internal_flag is intentionally unused to demonstrate
## the W_UNUSED lint rule. In production, you might waive signals
## that feed into optional debug logic.
##
## waive -rule {W_UNUSED}
##   -module {counter_with_issues}
##   -signal {internal_flag}
##   -comment "Tutorial example: intentionally demonstrates W_UNUSED"

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 3: Async reset not needed for test wrapper (example)
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## In some designs, test wrappers or simulation-only modules do not
## require asynchronous reset because they are only used in controlled
## test environments.
##
## waive -rule {W_REGS_ARST}
##   -module {test_wrapper}
##   -comment "Test wrapper operates in simulation only; async reset not required"

## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Waiver 4: Non-synthesizable constructs in simulation modules
## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## Simulation-only modules may legitimately use initial blocks, delays,
## and system tasks. Waive SYNTH_5130 for these modules.
##
## waive -rule {SYNTH_5130}
##   -module {tb_*}
##   -comment "Testbench modules are not synthesized"

##=============================================================================
## Template for Adding New Waivers
##=============================================================================
## Copy and fill in the template below for each new waiver:
##
## ## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## ## Waiver N: <Brief description>
## ## ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
## ## <Detailed justification — why is this safe?>
## ##
## waive -rule {RULE_ID}
##   -module {module_name}
##   -signal {signal_name}
##   -comment "Justification text"
